		{
			"id": "115707378280485130",
			"parentId": 0,
			"url": "https://front-end.social/@zzzzBov/115707378262279062",
			"author": {
				"name": "Timothy Leverett",
				"url": "https://front-end.social/@zzzzBov",
				"avatarUrl": "https://cdn.masto.host/fediversezachleatcom/cache/accounts/avatars/109/300/675/994/731/167/original/9bb12047449ddcc4.png"
			},
			"date": "2025-12-12T15:47:57.000Z",
			"content": "<p><span class=\"h-card\" translate=\"no\"><a href=\"https://fediverse.zachleat.com/@zachleat\" class=\"u-url mention\" rel=\"nofollow noopener\" target=\"_blank\">@<span>zachleat</span></a></span> absolutely yes. It's just that there's so much more of it that working software is unnoticeable, so we can only think of the times it fails us.</p><p>The barrier to entry is also much lower, so there are plenty of developers who are learning in production, and didn't benefit from the slow methodical practice of writing software back when hours were cheap and hardware was expensive. </p><p>We expect modern software engineers to do more with much less and it mostly works which is amazing.</p>",
			"via": "fediverse"
		},
